WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
Tokens: the unit you pay for.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.397
Models don’t read characters or words.

3
00:00:04.677 --> 00:00:13.275
They read tokens: chunks of text from a fixed vocabulary, typically tens of thousands to a few hundred thousand entries, learned from data.

4
00:00:13.555 --> 00:00:15.816
Common words are usually one token.

5
00:00:16.096 --> 00:00:21.621
Rare words, names, code identifiers, and non-English text get split into several pieces.

6
00:00:21.901 --> 00:00:34.829
OpenAI’s rule of thumb for English is about four characters, or three quarters of a word, per token; treat that as a rough estimate, because every model family uses its own tokenizer and the counts differ.

7
00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:38.053
Tokens matter to you as a builder for three reasons.

8
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:45.331
First, you are billed per token, separately for input and output, and output tokens usually cost several times more.

9
00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:51.819
Second, limits are expressed in tokens: the context window, and the maximum length of a response.

10
00:00:52.099 --> 00:00:55.043
Third, some odd failures come from tokenization.

11
00:00:55.323 --> 00:01:04.965
A model that sees “strawberry” as two or three chunks has no direct view of the individual letters, which is why letter-counting questions were a famous weak spot.
